 "findings": [
  {
   "severity": "MEDIUM",
   "title": "the pair is effectively untradeable on lopsided reserves",
   "detail": "the smallest swap producing even one mojo of output is 25,075,225,677,032, 25.1% of the reserve. A pool whose thin side has floored to single digits quotes a price but cannot be traded -- which is the state our own drained pools were left in, and the reason the burn cannot promise a standing market."
  },
  {
   "severity": "INFO",
   "title": "a single vault exit overcharges a large holder; splitting reaches the right price",
   "detail": "Splitting a vault exit into 100 removals recovers up to 99% of the fee, because the fee stays in the reserve and the holder re-claims their own share of it while exiting. This is ORDINARY AMM ECONOMICS: any LP who trades against their own pool pays the fee and receives their share back, so the net cost is fee x (1 - share). The rate is identical for everyone and proportional to trade size; what differs is how much of the pool you own. The wrinkle is that a SINGLE removal does not apply that rebate, so a 90% holder charged the full rate is overcharged 9x and splitting is the only way to reach the correct price. Vault-only: vault_fee_bps is 0 for n >= 2."
  },
  {
   "severity": "INFO",
   "title": "the two-sided vault charge is correct and stays",
   "detail": "A vault crossing is two trades -- in and out -- and costs 60.0 bps against a swap pool's ~60 bps for A->B->A, measured in _sim_v15_vault_consistency.py. Charging entry-only would price a vault crossing at half the equivalent swap. Fragmenting a DEPOSIT mints slightly less, so the asymmetry is redemption-only."
  }
